# Cloudflare Pages SPA routing.
# This one line is the whole reason for the migration: /learn is served as
# index.html with a 200, so the client router just works. On GitHub Pages the
# same request is a genuine 404 that has to be bounced through 404.html and a
# redirect shim, and that shim shipped broken, turning "/" + "/learn" into
# "//learn" which the browser read as a hostname. Every shared link died.
# A missing asset returns this file with a 200 and a text/html content type,
# which the browser refuses as a module script, so the failure mode of a missing
# bundle is a blank page rather than an error. That is also what a request lands
# on during the seconds after a deploy, when the edge still has the previous
# deployment and the HTML already names the new hashed filename.
#
# Two ways to make that miss honest were tried and both were measured on preview
# deployments rather than reasoned about:
#
#   /assets/* /404.html 404   with no such file: no effect at all, the rule is
#                             inert when its destination does not exist.
#   the same, plus a real public/404.html: the file's mere presence takes over
#                             routing, and /learn, /build, /writing and every
#                             /learn/<card> deep link returned 404. Confirmed on
#                             redirect-test2.sumitgundawar.pages.dev.
#
# So this catch-all stays exactly as it is. A 404.html cannot exist in this
# output while these routes are client-side, and that is the whole reason the
# migration off GitHub Pages happened.
/*    /index.html   200
